  "query_preview": "..Monsoon moisture will remain in place through Friday\nas the ridge responsible for this influx gradually shifts to the\neast. As this ridge shifts to the east a weak trough will push\ninto the western CONUS. This will enhance lift on Thursday and\nFriday to expand widespread convection across all of Utah and SW\nWyoming. The flash flooding risk will gradually expand into\nnorthern Utah Thursday and Friday with slot canyons, normal dry \nwashes, urban areas, and recent burn scars will be most \nsusceptible to flooding. \n\nConvection will develop each afternoon and persist through the\nearly evening before gradually diminishing overnight. The caveat\nwith this will be for the potential for nocturnal convection.\nGuidance continues to hint at lingering moisture overnight, such\nas tonight across northern Utah, but models struggle to capture\nthese features accurately. The most likely time for nocturnal\nactivity will come Thursday and Friday as forcing increases.\nTemperatures will also trend down 10-15 degrees with the increased\ncloud coverage and cooling temperatures aloft.\n\nSlightly drier air moves into the area this weekend with most of\nthe diurnal convection confined to the higher terrain. Moisture\nmoves back into the area next week as the ridge re-positions\nitself over the Four Corners. This active pattern looks to remain\nacross the area, in some form, through the next couple of weeks.",
